/-!
# The Bockstein complex of an invertible ideal

Let `X` be a ringed space, `I` an invertible sheaf of `O_X`-modules and `ι : I ⟶ O_X` a
monomorphism exhibiting `I` as an invertible ideal sheaf, and write `I^i` for the `i`-th
integer tensor power of `I`. For an arbitrary object `M` of `D(O_X)` the triangle
`M ⊗^L I^{i+1}/I^{i+2} → M ⊗^L I^i/I^{i+2} → M ⊗^L I^i/I^{i+1}` has a connecting map on
cohomology sheaves,
`β = β^i : H^i(M ⊗^L I^i/I^{i+1}) ⟶ H^{i+1}(M ⊗^L I^{i+1}/I^{i+2})`,
the *Bockstein operator*. Consecutive Bockstein operators compose to zero, so the sheaves
`H^i(M ⊗^L I^i/I^{i+1})` form a cochain complex `H^•(M/I)`, the *Bockstein complex*.

## Main definitions

* `quotientIncl`, `quotientProj`, `quotientShortComplex`: for two composable morphisms
  `a : A₃ ⟶ A₂` and `b : A₂ ⟶ A₁` in an abelian category, the sequence
  `coker a → coker (a ≫ b) → coker b`.
* `powerQuotient`, `powerQuotientShortComplex`: the quotient `I^i/I^{i+1}` and the
  successive-power sequence `I^{i+1}/I^{i+2} → I^i/I^{i+2} → I^i/I^{i+1}`.
* `bocksteinTerm`, `bocksteinOperator`: the cohomology sheaf `H^i(M ⊗^L I^i/I^{i+1})` and the
  Bockstein connecting map out of it.
* `bocksteinComplex`: the Bockstein complex `H^•(M/I)`.

## Main results

* `quotientShortComplex_shortExact`: `0 → coker a → coker (a ≫ b) → coker b → 0` is short
  exact when `a` and `b` are monomorphisms.
* `powerQuotientShortExact`: the successive-power sequence
  `0 → I^{i+1}/I^{i+2} → I^i/I^{i+2} → I^i/I^{i+1} → 0` is short exact.
* `bockstein_comp_bockstein` (Tag 0GT8): `β^{i+1} ∘ β^i = 0`.
* `bocksteinComplex_X` and `bocksteinComplex_d` identify the terms and the differentials of
  the Bockstein complex.

## Implementation notes

Situation 0GT4 asks that `I` be locally generated by a single nonzerodivisor. What is assumed
here is instead that `I` is invertible together with `Mono ι`, which is condition (2) of
Tag 0GT3; this is formally weaker, hence the statements below are formally stronger, and it is
the generalization sanctioned by the footnote to Situation 0GT4. It is also exactly the
hypothesis under which the décalage of Tag 0GT6 is developed in
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/Category/ModuleCat/Sheaf/EtaSheaf.lean`, whose `idealPowerIncl` supplies
the transition maps `I^{i+1} ⟶ I^i` used here.

The square-zero statement is isolated as a fact about an arbitrary chain of three composable
monomorphisms `A₄ → A₃ → A₂ → A₁` in an abelian category together with a triangulated
endofunctor `G` of its derived category, namely `delta_comp_delta`. This is the second of the
two proofs offered by the source: rather than reducing to stalks and citing More on Algebra,
Tag 0F7N, it works in `D(O_X)` directly. The connecting map of the quotient sequence of
`(a, b)` is factored, by naturality of the homology sequence along the map of short exact
sequences `(0 → A₂ → A₁ → coker b → 0) ⟶ (0 → coker a → coker (a ≫ b) → coker b → 0)`,
through the map induced by `coker.π a`; that map in turn factors through the surjection
`coker (c ≫ a) → coker a` of the next quotient sequence, and a connecting map kills the image
of the second map of its own triangle. No property of `I` beyond monicity of its transition
maps enters, which is why the fact is stated for a bare chain of monomorphisms.

The successive-quotient sequence is `kernelCokernelCompSequence` of the two monomorphisms,
whose middle three terms it extracts; its outer terms vanish precisely because `a` and `b` are
monomorphisms.

Monicity of `idealPowerIncl X I ι n O_X` is reproved here rather than taken from Tag 0GT6:
the version there needs `I`-torsion-freeness of the module being twisted, whereas for the
structure sheaf itself the map is the image of `ι` under the tensor autoequivalence attached to
the invertible sheaf `I^{⊗ n}`, so monicity follows from invertibility alone. Invertibility is
converted between the `Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible` spelling of Tag 01CS, which the
décalage uses, and the `AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible` spelling, which the
tensor-power calculus of Tag 01CU uses, by `IsInvertible.ofAlgebraicGeometry`.

The derived tensor product is not redefined here: it is
`Stacks.DerivedTensor.RingedSite.canonicalDerivedTensorProduct X.sheaf`, the construction of
Tag 06YU at the ringed site of opens of `X`. Its existence needs additivity of the sheaf tensor
product in each variable and the total complexes of the tensor double complexes; those are the
instances that `StacksFrontier/Topology/Sheaves/Cohomology/KFlat/OnARingedSpace.lean` registers for
a ringed space, which is why that module is imported.

The hypotheses `I`, `ι` and the invertibility instance are written out on every declaration
rather than collected in a `variable` block. As section variables they make
`powerQuotientShortComplex` exceed the heartbeat budget, and the declarations that do elaborate
are not axiom-clean; with explicit binders neither happens.

